00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.070 align:start position:0%
 
the white house is keeping a close eye  on ukraine as about a 100 000 russian  troops sit at russia's border with  ukraine and while diplomacy is the  preferred solution president biden today  spoke in plain language on the  consequences russia would face if  vladimir putin were to direct a move on  ukraine  if any  any assembled russian units move across  ukrainian border  that is an invasion  but it will be met with severe and  coordinated economic response that i've  discussed in detail with our allies as  well as laid out very clearly for  president putin  but there is no doubt let there be no  doubt at all that if putin makes this  choice russia will pay a heavy price  all right so given biden's remarks we  also want to talk about what russia's  strategy is here and what might play out  if russian forces do cross the ukrainian  border so joining us to discuss is  melinda herring deputy director of the  atlantic council's eurasia center  melinda welcome to quick take you  obviously just heard this conversation  with president biden saying that russia  will pay a heavy price do you think his  remarks uh made things worse  no i think that  the kremlin's just going to shrug his  shoulders uh president biden has made a  lot of statements he told the kremlin  that he will do three things if they  invade ukraine again you will see the  meanest nastiest package of sanctions  that we've ever thrown at you you will  see more defensive material in ukraine  and you will see more nato troops in  eastern europe so i don't think this is  anything new he's using you know he's  trying to scare putin uh the question is  has he  does moscow fear this package of  sanctions i don't know that it does  did he misspeak though because the white  house is trying to walk things back a  little bit here  joe biden really screwed up last night  he made a statement and he took off his  presidential hat and he put on his think  tank cap he was trying to do my job he  was trying to analyze the situation and  he made a number of screw-ups he said he  thinks that putin is going to go in he  said that there's different scenarios uh  he said that there would be different  responses he talked about differences  between the allies this is no no no no  no the white house immediately tried to  step it back and issue you know a  regular talking points that you would  expect the white house uh to issue that  we have ukraine's back that our position  hasn't changed but you know putting um  say he basically seeded the initiative  to putin and said that the ball is in  your court uh and that's a really  dangerous place to be in but it's not  game over scarlet there's a chance that  the white house could uh pull back that  initiative if they do something really  smart what do they need to do they need  to push nato to put troops in eastern  europe right now before vladimir putin  does anything more serious and then also  to keep sending defensive material to  ukraine now that that's what would  convince putin that the u.s is serious  about deterrence what do you think the  likelihood is of the us successfully  pushing nato on getting troops on the  ground there given that the u.s has said  we we won't be sending troops ourselves  i think nato is open to it there's  discussions right now  that are underway and i think it's a  real possibility if the white house  stands up and shows leadership on this  issue today  you know one thing about this whole  situation is we've been here before it's  been a while right because russian  troops have been gathering at the  ukraine border since november why is the  u.s why is the ukraine so convinced that  putin will be moving the troops across  the border by late january early  february when they could stay there  indefinitely  they can't stay there indefinitely that  that's one uh problem that i'd like to  correct so in order to keep these troops  there it takes a lot of money and  material and they can't stay um at these  at these temporary bases you know  forever and ever there's a limited time  window uh the weather is not on putin's  side it has been a  uh it's been a mild winter and if he  wants to do a full land scale invasion  he needs he needs the the mud to freeze  so that the tanks can roll in but you  know what i i just got off the phone  with some folks in kiev and they've said  that you all in washington need to calm  down vladimir putin is not doing a land  invasion anytime soon uh the latest  number from ukraine uh my ukrainian uh  defense people say that there are 120  000 to and forty thousand russian troops  and they're in the north they're in the  east and they're in the south so they  surround ukraine by three sides but if  you wanted to do a full-scale land  invasion you would need at least 200 000  troops but 300 000 would be more  preferable and the russians aren't doing  the things that you would need to do  they are putting the logistics staff in  place right now if if they were planning  a full-scale land invasion so  he also said watch  the movement of people and watch the  movement of fuel these are two things  that military analysts will be watching  in the weeks to come  that's so interesting that's so  interesting that they said that you guys  are misunderstanding a lot of this what  else are we misunderstanding in the us  when it comes to putin's intentions and  and how things appear  so i i think we underestimate him at  first i thought this conflict was all  about ukraine i thought vladimir putin  wants to destroy the western project but  this is a lot this is about much more  than ukraine ukraine is a lever putin  wants to destroy nato he wants to make  the west look weak and he wants to  rewrite the the the rules of the  european security architecture so when  we want to just sort of ignore this  problem and go back to trying to fix  covid or thinking about climate change  or some of the other administration's  priorities we can't vladimir putin is  not going away even if we want to ignore  him he is super ambitious and he's not  going to be satisfied even if he gets  what he wants in ukraine given that what  would you say is the role of the rest of  europe here  europe needs to step up that that's  that's the issue uh the germans have  refused to sell weapons to ukraine knock  it off  it's obvious that the ukrainians need  weapons and they need support ukraine uh  europe needs to speak in a unified voice  it needs to send as much material as it  can and it needs to maintain its unity  that that's the really crucial point  right now and it can't sell ukraine out  it cannot engage in any kind of deal  that we will protect our security in  place of ukraine that's what russia  wants russia wants uh europe to trade  ukraine away that that is a no-go  it very much feels like a chess match  between joe biden who often misspeaks on  this as we've seen and vladimir putin  who you've just mentioned we tend to  underestimate  we know that a bipartisan delegation of  senators traveled to ukraine over the  weekend to try to reassure kiev that the  u.s stood by and stood with them is  there a role for other groups to play  here or does it really come down to how  biden and putin decide on their next  step  that's a great question so vladimir  putin wants to narrow the negotiating  table he wants the table to be vladimir  putin and joe biden but if biden was  smart last week he kept expanding it so  we had the osce we had nato involved you  can't decide ukraine's future without  europe and without the ukrainians and  that's a point the white house has been  really really consistent on and i i i  would applaud them for that  i'm a little curious as well just about  the individuals living in ukraine  melinda how concerned are you for for  them  do you see this becoming you know a  situation like anything else that we've  seen uh the biden administration  handling in terms of foreign policy  efforts to help other nations handle  human rights abuses if things get to  that point  so the mood in kiev is very different  than it is in washington i live in  washington and it's ukraine ukraine  ukraine all the time right now it's the  biggest crisis  and i think the white house deserves  credit uh for  uh getting europe and ukraine uh waking  everyone up and saying guys this is  really a big crisis it could spiral into  a world war and that's i'm saying that  without exaggeration it really could um  but ukrainians feel differently uh i  have a stringer in kiev now he just  wrote a wonderful piece for the atlantic  council website his name is vladislav  davidson and he says that people either  don't want to talk about it hey we've  been at war for eight years  nothing new or they have a sense of  fatalism if russia's going to attack  it's going to attack so  the mood in kiev is is really one um  that you that i certainly didn't expect  it's a mix of denial and fatalism  that is so interesting i i go back to  what maddie had said earlier which is  the u.s made clear that u.s troops would  not be called in but do you see any  scenario melinda in which the u.s  military might be called in to to take  on what's happening in the ukraine and  with russia  i don't think that joe biden  can afford politically to send troops  there and he he said it's no way but  there are other things that we could do  we can send more trainers uh we can send  more ships uh there's a number of things  that we could do to bolster ukraine uh  and to show that we're serious about  deterrence short of sending boots on the  ground  when a final question for you you  mentioned that you're in washington and  that this is the topic of the hour of  the day long term do you think that this  is going to be domestically a voting  issue and at the ballot issue a kitchen  table issue for voters and and is that  perhaps why biden is pushing it so much  right now  yes and no so this is an election year  and joe biden's numbers are sliding  right and he had a big screw-up in  afghanistan he cannot afford another  major foreign policy blunder remember  joe biden was mr senate foreign  relations committee he has a lot of  expertise on foreign policy so he really  can't uh afford another screw-up and  covet hasn't gone well and the economy  hasn't turned around so uh you know his  he's he's got to get this one right and  he also has a personal stake in in  ukraine you have to remember this when  he was vice president he was negotiating  he was going back and forth all the time  he really really cares about how things  land in ukraine he wants ukraine to  succeed so i think he has a personal  stake and politically he's got to get it  right  it's a lot of things that he has to get  right then melinda thank you so much for  the context on the situation escalating  in ukraine melinda herring is deputy  director of the atlantic council's  eurasia center

